  "Question": "What is the correct ordering of the three numbers \\( \\frac{5}{19} ,\\) \\( \\frac{7}{21} ,\\) and \\( \\frac{9}{23} ,\\) in increasing order?",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\( \\dfrac{9}{23} \\lt \\dfrac{7}{21} \\lt \\dfrac{5}{19}\\)",
    "B": "\\( \\dfrac{5}{19} \\lt \\dfrac{7}{21} \\lt\n\\dfrac{9}{23}\\)",
    "C": "\\(\\dfrac{9}{23} \\lt \\dfrac{5}{19} \\lt \\dfrac{7}{21} \\)",
    "D": "\\( \\dfrac{5}{19} \\lt \\dfrac{9}{23} \\lt \\dfrac{7}{21}\\)",
    "E": "\\(\\dfrac{7}{21} \\lt \\dfrac{5}{19} \\lt \\dfrac{9}{23} \\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "B",
  "Solution": "The fraction \\(\\frac{7}{21}=\\frac13\\) gives a useful benchmark. For fractions with the same positive numerator, a larger positive denominator gives a smaller fraction.\n\nSince \\(19>15\\),\n\\[\n\\frac{5}{19}<\\frac{5}{15}=\\frac13.\n\\]\nSince \\(23<27\\),\n\\[\n\\frac{9}{23}>\\frac{9}{27}=\\frac13.\n\\]\nTherefore the increasing order is\n\\[\n\\frac{5}{19}<\\frac{7}{21}<\\frac{9}{23},\n\\]\nwhich is choice \\(\\text{B}\\).",
